    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a great start, thank you. Some of my more skeptical students might argue that this idea is just a way for judges to invent new rights that aren't actually in the Constitution, rather than following the law as it's written. How should I address those criticisms in the lesson so they understand why substantive due process is still considered a necessary part of our legal system?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "This \"judge-made law\" criticism is common, as the evolution of this doctrine is often debated among legal scholars. To address this, you can explain that proponents argue it is not \"invention,\" but rather the judicial *interpretation* of the broad, foundational concept of \"liberty\" guaranteed in the Due Process clause. They argue that the Constitution must be a *living document* capable of protecting evolving fundamental rights in a changing society, not just a rigid rulebook from the 18th century.",
      "source": "target",
